{
  "$comment": [
    "THE PINNED SPECIFICATION for a card's identity. This file is the",
    "contract between two independent implementations: card_identity.c,",
    "which runs on the ATtiny1616 and writes the tag, and the host side in",
    "pond/src/card/, which records the same card in cards.csv and verifies",
    "its claims.",
    "",
    "If those two disagree by one bit of ordering, EVERY card is unknown to",
    "the server at once - and it fails quietly, because an unknown serial is",
    "designed to degrade rather than error. So neither side is tested",
    "against the other. Both are tested against this file.",
    "",
    "Regenerate with: shared/firmware/card-identity/regenerate.sh",
    "Never edit the vectors by hand - that would make this agree with",
    "whatever is currently broken."
  ],
  "version": 1,

  "serial": {
    "input": "SIGROW.SERNUM, all ten bytes, in the order the chip presents them",
    "derivation": "crockford40(SipHash-2-4(SERIAL_KEY, sernum) & 0xFFFFFFFFFF)",
    "serial_key_ascii": "ducky.serial.v1",
    "serial_key_ascii_note": "15 characters; the 16th key byte is 0x00 - see serial_key_hex for the exact bytes",
    "serial_key_hex": "6475636b792e73657269616c2e763100",
    "serial_key_is_public": true,
    "alphabet": "0123456789ABCDEFGHJKMNPQRSTVWXYZ",
    "length": 8,
    "bit_order": "most significant group first, 5 bits per character",
    "why_hashed_not_sliced": [
      "BUILD-PLAN originally said 'the low 40 bits' of SERNUM. SERNUM is lot",
      "number, wafer number and die coordinates - a hundred cards from one",
      "reel share a lot, so any fixed 40-bit slice is partly CONSTANT across",
      "the batch. The '4 in a billion' collision estimate assumes 40 uniform",
      "bits and would not have been true of the thing it described.",
      "Hashing all ten bytes uses every bit of entropy the chip has and",
      "makes that estimate honest. See the 'reel sibling' vectors below:",
      "two chips one die coordinate apart produce unrelated serials."
    ]
  },

  "token": {
    "purpose": "the card SIGNS its claim - an unseen counter is not a credential",
    "message": "the 8 ASCII serial characters, then the counter as 2 bytes LITTLE-ENDIAN; 10 bytes, no separator, no NUL",
    "derivation": "hex(SipHash-2-4(FIRMWARE_SECRET, message) & 0xFFFFFFFFFF)",
    "length": 10,
    "case": "lowercase",
    "bit_order": "most significant nibble first",
    "test_key_hex": "000102030405060708090a0b0c0d0e0f",
    "test_key_is_not_the_real_secret": "FIRMWARE_SECRET never enters this repository; see secrets.h.example"
  },

  "counter": {
    "width_bits": 16,
    "url_form": "4 lowercase hex characters",
    "rule": "increment EEPROM FIRST, then write the NDEF - the other order lets a consumed counter reappear, which is the one outcome that breaks the scheme"
  },

  "url": {
    "template": "https://ducky.davidyang.work/?d=<digit>&c=<serial>&g=<counter>&t=<token>",
    "digit_is_first": "so the one byte the MCU patches per fortune never moves"
  },

